{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q002_mazg_hise_remote_homology","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 (current release, per /api/v1/) places d1vmga_ and d1y6xa1 in the same superfamily, a.204.1 "all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases", but in different families. d1vmga_ (1vmg chain A; sunid 113676; a.204.1.2) is in the "MazG-like" family (sunid 116995's protein parent 116994 is annotated as a Sulfolobus solfataricus hypothetical protein, SSo12199/SSo3215). d1y6xa1 (1y6x chain A residues 7-93; sunid 122676; a.204.1.4) is in the "HisE-like (PRA-PH)" family, whose protein node is phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase HisE from Mycobacterium tuberculosis. SCOPe therefore supports a remote, superfamily-level evolutionary relationship (common ancestry of the NTP-pyrophosphatase fold, all-alpha class a, fold a.204) and does not support a closer family-level or orthologous relationship. The SCOPe superfamily call rests on structural and functional-similarity criteria, not on a sequence-identity claim, so no percent identity should be inferred from it.

Functional conclusions that go beyond this evidence: (1) that d1vmga_ has HisE-type phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase activity or participates in histidine biosynthesis; (2) that d1vmga_ is a bona fide MazG nucleotide pyrophosphohydrolase, because its SCOPe protein-level annotation is only 'hypothetical protein' and the family label describes structural similarity, not enzymatic assignment; (3) that the two domains share substrate specificity, catalytic residues, metal or cofactor requirements, or reaction chemistry; (4) that the whole HisE protein or the whole MazG-like protein behaves like these domains, since d1y6xa1 covers only residues 7-93 of 1y6x chain A and d1vmga_ is a single chain-level domain; and (5) that any member of a.204.1.2 or a.204.1.4 can be used as a template or representative for the other family without further evidence.

To make any function transfer, SCOPe is not enough. Required external evidence includes a sequence or profile alignment with explicit catalytic-motif comparison, a structure-based active-site comparison (for example, ligand-bound structures of each family), curated functional annotations (for example UniProt or PDB ligand records), and experimental activity or mutagenesis data for the MazG-like domain. Representative selection should use one domain per family here (1vmg A and 1y6x A 7-93), and conclusions should be limited to the superfamily-level remote relationship.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge (HisE as phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase; MazG as an NTP pyrophosphohydrolase family, used only to frame the question)","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1vmga_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1y6xa1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/113676","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/122676","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/116995","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/116994","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/116993","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/140801","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/140798","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/140797","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/101386"]}]}
